  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 223 PM CST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nThe large scale pattern towards the end of the work week features a \nbuilding upper-level ridge over the northwestern CONUS, while the \ntrough departing Iowa enters the Ohio Valley region and becomes one \nwith a larger and rather potent trough sweeping across the upper \nGreat Lakes region. The net result will be upper level northwest \nflow becoming dominant over Iowa, leading to a general cooler and \nquiet pattern. Highs will range only in the 40s, with overnight lows \nin the 20s to 30s through Friday. A few models suggests a low \npotential for light rain showers into eastern Iowa as wrap around \nprecipitation may extend its influence westwards into Iowa as the \nlow pressure deepens over the Ohio Valley region Thursday, though \nthis would be battling a strong push of dry air as surface high \npressure descends from Canada into the Midwest. Just worth a casual \nmention at this time, but something to keep an eye on for possible \ninclusion if the drier air is slower to arrive. While the \naforementioned upper ridge nears the central US into the \nweekend, northwest flow with its dry and cooler air will \ngenerally hold. Highs over the weekend as a result will remain \nmore towards seasonal norms in the 40s to 50s, with lows in the \n20s to 30s.",
